    "en": {
      "title": "Regular and irregular verbs",
      "summary": "Regular verbs add -ed; irregular verbs must be learned.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "A regular verb forms its past and past participle with -ed. An irregular verb has other forms that you must learn."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "walk, **walked**, **walked**",
                "go, **went**, **gone**",
                "see, **saw**, **seen**"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Most English verbs are regular, but many of the most common ones are irregular. Irregular verbs are usually learned as three forms: base, past and past participle."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Base</th><th>Past</th><th>Past participle</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>work</strong></td><td><strong>worked</strong></td><td><strong>worked</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>buy</strong></td><td><strong>bought</strong></td><td><strong>bought</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>write</strong></td><td><strong>wrote</strong></td><td><strong>written</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>cut</strong></td><td><strong>cut</strong></td><td><strong>cut</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
